          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life</strong><br>\nCamille Pissarro was born on July 10, 1830, on the island of St. Thomas, which was then part of the Danish West Indies. He moved to Paris to study art, even though his family expected him to enter the family business. Pissarro became a key figure in Impressionist and Post-Impressionist movements.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Artistic Beginnings</strong><br>\nPissarro studied at various academies, including the École des Beaux-Arts and Académie Suisse. His early work reflected the influence of the rural scenes of French countryside life, which remained a recurrent theme throughout his career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impressionism and Rebellion</strong><br>\nAs a founding member of the Impressionist movement, Pissarro was the only artist to display his works in all eight Paris Impressionist exhibitions. He was dedicated to the movement's innovative techniques and ideas, rebelling against the conventional art of the time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
