          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence on Other Artists</strong><br>\nPissarro was a mentor to many younger artists, including Cézanne and Gauguin, and his discussions on the theories behind Impressionism influenced their development as artists. His collaboration with these artists also allowed him to explore new ideas and techniques.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Evolution</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Pissarro's work evolved to include urban scenes. He painted series of Parisian landscapes, capturing the city in various conditions of weather and light, showcasing his evolving style and persistent exploration of the effects of atmosphere in art.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nCamille Pissarro died in Paris on November 13, 1903. His legacy is that of a 'painter's painter,' revered by his contemporaries for his commitment to painting from nature and his role as an elder statesman of Impressionism and mentor to the Post-Impressionists.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
